# Standards Can Shape The Direction of Programming Languages

**and** guide implementers in a positive direction for their users



## Some Downsides

- Timelag -- it takes time for changes to proliferate
- Friction -- some features are a constant fight
- Implementer Expectations -- need to be conscientious and appreciative of implementer time



## Good Benefits

- Implementations know what to spend their time on
- Users know how to voice their concern
- Shared language of implementation and specification
- **Liability Coverage for Software/Hardware Behaviors**



## .... Liability? Huh??

Yes! Specifications and Standards can be leveraged by organizations to  
guarantee either behavioral boundaries or specific actions!

Heavily used for:

- C++
- C
- Java
- Fortran
- Ada (and more!)



## Having a Standard Means...

It can be certified and held accountable/responsible, and also offloads liability!

- IEC 61508 and their cerification/accreditation bodies,  
  as well as the Safety Integrity Level (SIL)
- ISO 26262 & Automotive Safety Integrity Level (ASIL)



## Even Rust (sort of) Needed a Standard

Ferrocene is not THE Rust Standard™, but it IS a  
safety-certified spinoff of Rust which hammers out  
much of the behavior!

[![A picture of the Ferrocene repository.](source/img/ferrocene-repo.png)](https://ferrocene.dev/)
